A||||4||pHoOl||Lock problems||||1||
Z||000000||pHoOl||11-30-2000||11:10 AM||||This hasn't been a problem until it started getting cold this winter.  I'll stick the key in the door and turn the key.  First off, it's kinda hard getting it in the driver's side door.  (passenger works fine)  When i do finally get the key in, turn it and try to take it out, it won't come out.  I usually have to sit there and shake it for three or four minutes trying to jiggle it just the right way to get it out.<P><BR>What should i squirt in there to fix this.  It's not always happening when it's below freezing, but when it gets kinda cold it happens.  I'm not sure if lock de-icer will work since it happens before it gets down to freezing.  I was also thinking about WD-40.  Any ideas?  Anything that i could possibly damage by putting any of this stuff in?<P>thanks all!!<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.gvsuphool.homestead.com/files/pHoOl_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Tigger||11-30-2000||11:19 AM||||WD-40 shouldn't hurt anything.  Try flushing the cylinder with WD-40 first.  Chances are grit and dirt has built up inside.  If that's no help, let me know, I've rebuilt the lock cylinder on mine.<P>------------------<BR>T-T-F-N, Tah Tah For Now!<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/tiggerblue2.gif">  <p>[This message has been edited by Tigger (edited 11-30-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||GodSend||11-30-2000||11:24 AM||||  I don't recommend using WD-40, It attracts dust really bad and will just end up making things worse in the long run.  There are two things you can try... either Lock Grafight (obviously that is not the correct spelling) or get some lubricant which is safe for locks (One of he ones that fights rust, and isent washed off by water) - It comes in a spray can similar to WD-40, but it is alot more perminant..(sprays for rusted bolts are good too)||||reg||1
Z||000003||Tigger||11-30-2000||11:43 AM||||Yea, for the long run that would be better.  I was thinking if all you had handy was WD, I'd use it just to try to flush any major crud out.||||reg||1
Z||000004||PlasticWrapd||11-30-2000||12:20 PM||||A locksmith told me this one....  Get a can of "Brakleen" brake parts cleaner.  CRC makes it and it's composed mostly of trichlorethane which evaporates with no residue.  Cover and protect your paint or remove the lock cylinder from the car.  Use it with the tube attaced to the spray nozzle and soak the inside of the lock through the keyhole.  Do it several times, allowing time for the cleaner to evaporate.  After the lock has dried out, lubricate it with a little graphite.  Not too much or your key will turn black every time you unlock the car.  If you don't have graphite, you can rub a #2 pencil all over the key or rub the #2 pencil on a nail file to get powdered graphite to put in the lock.  <P>** Winter hint **  if your lock is frozen and the key won't go in or turn, heat the key with a butane lighter before inserting it in the lock.  Works great in a pinch.||||reg||1
